  "title": "Leverage Dynamics and Commercial Posture under Proposed US-EU Trade Framework",
  "question": "Who gains and loses leverage under the proposed US-EU trade framework, and what commercial posture should a mid-sized exporter adopt?",
  "decision": "Calibrate commercial hedging, supply chain contracting, and regulatory compliance posture for a mid-sized exporter without making irreversible capital commitments prior to finalized legal text.",
  "audience": "Executive leadership and commercial strategy team of a mid-sized exporter operating between the US and EU",
  "time_horizon": "6 to 18 months (negotiation through prospective initial implementation phase)",
  "mode": "B",
  "evidence_mode": "user-provided sources",
  "bottom_line": {
    "text": "The proposed US-EU trade framework shifts relative bargaining power toward large multinational incumbents and regulatory bodies capable of navigating harmonized conformity rules, while mid-sized exporters risk margin pressure and asymmetric compliance pass-throughs from key buyers. Mid-sized exporters should adopt a flexible, dual-track commercial posture: audit supply chain compliance preparedness and insert conditional renegotiation clauses into transatlantic contracts without making irreversible capital investments ahead of finalized legal ratification.",
